Yeah. The useful move is to stop making Cage carry every kind of funny. Build an ensemble where each character has a different comedy engine, then let those engines collide.

I wouldn’t duplicate South Park’s exact voice, but we can use the broader ingredients: blunt dialogue, merciless escalation, social satire, characters taking idiotic premises completely seriously, and occasional moments of disturbing clarity.

Here’s a cast I’d play with:

* Fictionalized Steven Seagal — The Unmovable Expert. Knows everything, according to Steven Seagal. Never runs. Barely turns his torso. Somehow claims he trained whichever indigenous people, intelligence agency, animal species, or historical civilization becomes relevant. Someone mentions hippos: “I actually studied river combat with hippos in ’87.” Nobody believes him. Occasionally he is inexplicably right.
* Werner Herzog — The Prophet of Needless Darkness. Everyone else sees a vending machine. Herzog sees “a fluorescent altar at which modern man exchanges silver for salted despair.” Fantastic because he makes trivial things existentially horrifying while remaining completely calm.
* Jeff Goldblum — The Chaotic Naturalist. Fascinated by everything, incapable of reaching a noun efficiently. “The hippo is, ah—yes—quite a proposition, isn’t it?” He actually understands the science but becomes erotically intrigued by the concept of a municipal drainage system.
* Christopher Walken — The Broken Metronome. Not necessarily crazy; he simply places emphasis where no normal human would. He can turn instructions for assembling IKEA furniture into a hostage negotiation.
* Dale Varnish — Original character: Municipal Shaman. Forty-eight-year-old assistant manager of a Virginia strip mall who believes zoning regulations encode the ancient structure of reality. Usually wrong. Knows an alarming amount about storm drains. Keeps becoming essential.
* Dr. Patrice Coupon — Hypercompetent Idiot. Has three PhDs, can solve nonlinear differential equations on a napkin, yet repeatedly loses arguments to automatic doors. Her comedy is the gap between extraordinary intelligence and spectacularly poor situational judgment.
* Terry “The Commissioner” Blevins — Bureaucratic Warlord. Treats tiny administrative powers like imperial sovereignty. He runs a homeowners association or youth soccer league as though commanding NATO. “You can ignore me, Gary, but you cannot ignore Article IV regarding exterior mulch.”
* Father Kevin — Cheerfully Underqualified Priest. Keeps being dragged into supernatural situations and really wishes everyone would stop assuming he knows how any of it works. “I’m a parish priest, Nicolas. I do weddings. Why are you handing me an Egyptian mirror?”
* Ronnie Two-Lunches — Pure appetite character. Completely unaffected by whatever cosmic or political catastrophe is occurring because he is trying to determine whether the buffet includes crab legs. His stupidity periodically cuts through everyone else’s overthinking.
* The Child Who Sees the Obvious Thing. Eleven years old. Doesn’t understand half the adult context, but routinely solves the central problem because everyone else is trapped inside elaborate narratives. Adults refuse to listen because his solution sounds insulting.

The important thing is the collision matrix.

Put Cage + Seagal together and you get competing mythologies: Cage discovers patterns; Seagal claims he already mastered them.

Cage + Herzog gives you manic wonder versus cosmic despair.

Goldblum + Herzog gives you: “Nature is astonishing” versus “Nature wants us dead.”

Dr. Coupon + Dale Varnish gives you formal intelligence versus accidental folk wisdom.

The Commissioner + literally anybody produces institutional satire.

And then the child walks into the room and says, “Why don’t you just unplug it?”

That gives you several different kinds of humor instead of recursively producing more Nicolas Cage.

For example, imagine the group discovers a secret government facility underneath a Bass Pro Shops.

Cage: “The aquarium was never decorative.”

Seagal: “Obviously. I helped design this facility.”

Dr. Coupon: “It opened in 2017.”

Seagal: “The public one.”

Herzog, staring at a largemouth bass: “He circles endlessly beneath artificial daylight, unaware that freedom was ever possible.”

Goldblum: “Well, ah, Werner, technically he was captive-bred, so freedom may be more of an inherited abstraction—”

Herzog: “Even worse.”

Dale Varnish: “Guys.”

Everyone ignores him.

Dale: “Guys.”

The Commissioner: “Nobody speaks until we establish jurisdiction.”

Dale points behind them.

“The elevator says SECRET LAB.”

They turn.

There is, in fact, a button labeled SECRET LAB.

Cage whispers, “Too easy.”

The eleven-year-old presses it.

Cage: “NO.”

Doors close.

Seagal folds his arms.

“I was about to do that.”

That ensemble could get much funnier because now Cage doesn’t have to be the joke every time. Sometimes he’s the straight man looking at someone even more insane than he is.